  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Clients and employers pay for proven skill and reliability. They judge you by evidence from your side test rather than by intentions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is writing down what you want instead of your current job, in concrete terms.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and leaving the corporate sector: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools help research options, draft proposals and handle admin. Generic plans sound alike.
  </p>
  <p>
    Judgment, savings discipline and relationships hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI to compare options; later, own the decision and its risks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general leaving the corporate sector path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Stay and adjust the role</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Low risk</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Slower change</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Move to another employer</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Fresh start</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Same sector risks</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Leave the sector</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Largest change</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs a tested plan</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn leaving the corporate sector cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Leaving the corporate sector is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM lessons.</li>
    <li>Free YouTube lessons and public documentation.</li>
    <li>Free practice datasets and open software.</li>
    <li>A senior or teacher who reviews your sample.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In twelve weeks, write your reason, test an alternative on the side, talk to three people and decide with a buffer.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

